{
  "version": 2,
  "hash": "374afbe8bf676be27bb086906724813a",
  "file_hash": "374afbe8bf676be27bb086906724813a",
  "translated_at": "2026-09-01T06:29:21.165935",
  "target_lang": "en",
  "chunks": [
    {
      "hash": "ee41670a2b967908211c51aaede28acf",
      "translation": "# Unified Control Plane (scope)\n\n> [!NOTE]  \n> This feature requires ErisPulse **2.8.0+**.\n\nThe unified control plane answers five questions: **Which modules are available, who receives which events, who can execute a specific command, what text a module processes, and which implementation parameters are overridden**. The control authority is entirely given to the user: declared at the **upper level** of module / adapter / command / processor registration (via configuration `ErisPulse.scope` or runtime `sdk.scope`), and the event pipeline automatically reads and executes at each level.\n\nThe control plane consolidates the previously separate permission systems, serving as the **sole entry point** for permission and access control in version 2.8.0:\n\n| Dimension | Controls What | Rejection Behavior | Configuration Path |\n|-----------|---------------|--------------------|---------------------|\n| **① Module** | Which modules are available (platform / Bot / session three levels) | Silent ignore (no reply, no claim) | `scope.platforms / bots / sessions` |\n| **② Identity** | Whether to receive events (adapter / Bot / session / user four levels) | Complete discard at entry (silent) | `scope.identity.*` |\n| **③ Command** | Who can execute a specific command (command names support glob) | Reply with \"insufficient permissions\" (explicit) | `scope.commands` |\n| **④ Processor** | Filter event processors of a module by text | Do not trigger (silent) | `scope.handlers` |\n| **⑤ Override** | Override implementation parameters of modules/commands (master/hidden/aliases/prefix) | —— (only changes parameters) | `scope.overrides` |\n\n{!--< tips >!--}\n1. Import the singleton via `from ErisPulse.Core import scope` (same object as `sdk.scope`)\n2. `scope.is_allowed(platform, bot_id, module, session_id)` checks if the module is available\n3. `scope.is_identity_allowed(platform, bot_id, session_id, user_id)` checks if the event is allowed\n4. `scope.allow_user(\"roll*\", platform, uid)` / `deny_user(...)` command ACL (supports glob)\n5. `scope.override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\", master=True)` overrides implementation parameters\n6. `scope.get_stats()` retrieves filtering statistics; `scope.get_topology()` views the five-dimensional topology\n{!--< /tips >!--}"
    },
    {
      "hash": "20701184cb2ef4b82c0b458cf4f3a834",
      "translation": "## Matching Entry Syntax (Unified Across the System)\n\nAll \"name lists\" in the control plane (module names, identity keys, command names) use the same matching syntax (`ErisPulse.Core.text_match`):\n\n| Syntax | Example | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| Exact name | `\"Chat\"` | Full value comparison, **case-insensitive** |\n| Glob | `\"Tool*\"` or `\"spam_*\"` | `*` matches any string / `?` matches a single character / `[seq]` matches a character set, case-insensitive |\n| Regular expression | `\"re:^Danger.*\"` | Declared with the `re:` prefix, uses regular expression `search` matching, default case-insensitive |\n\n- Invalid regular expressions **silently degrade** to \"no match\" (no error or crash)\n- Decorator parameters (`pattern=` / `regex=`) have fixed semantics: `pattern` is a glob, `regex` is the raw regular expression source (without the `re:` prefix); regular expression entries in control plane configurations **must** include the `re:` prefix"
    },
    {
      "hash": "f9a9492375f48bd0f810e546533e798e",
      "translation": "## Global Fallback: `default_allow`\n\n`default_allow` is the **only global** fallback switch (default: `true`), which uniformly applies to the three decision dimensions:\n\n- **Module dimension**: If no binding is matched → `default_allow` determines whether to allow or deny\n- **Identity dimension**: If no policy is matched → `default_allow` determines whether to allow or deny\n- **Command dimension**: If no ACL is configured → `default_allow=true` delegates to the developer's default permission chain; `false` (strict mode) means commands without configured ACL are denied\n\nSetting it to `false` enables the \"implicit denial\" strict mode: whitelist-based management, where **everything not explicitly allowed is denied**."
    },
    {
      "hash": "9293bffd2aaef7e5224239d97d4c4b16",
      "translation": "## Configuration File\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope]\ndefault_allow = true        # Global fallback (false = explicit deny strict mode)\ncache_size = 1024           # LRU cache size\n\n# ── ① Module-level (priority: session > Bot > platform) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.platforms.onebot11]\nmodules = [\"Chat\", \"Tool*\"]   # Whitelist: exact name / glob / re: regex\nblocked = [\"re:^Danger\"]\n[ErisPulse.scope.bots.onebot11.\"123456\"]\nmodules = [\"Chat\"]\n[ErisPulse.scope.sessions.onebot11.\"789012345\"]\nmodules = [\"Chat\"]\n\n# ── ② Identity-level (priority: user > session > Bot > adapter) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.adapters.onebot11]\ndeny = true                   # Drop all events from this entire adapter\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.bots.onebot11.\"123456\"]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.sessions.onebot11.\"g_blocked\"]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.users.onebot11]\nallow = [\"u_admin\"]           # User keys support glob / re: regex\ndeny = [\"u_bad\", \"spam_*\"]\n\n# ── ③ Command-level (command names support glob) ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.commands.\"roll*\"]\nallow = [\"onebot11:u_vip\"]    # User identifier \"platform:user_id\"\ndeny = [\"onebot11:u_bad\"]\n\n# ── ④ Handler/Text-level ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.handlers.MyModule]\npattern = \"签到*\"             # AND with code-side pattern/regex conditions\nregex = \"re:\\\\d+\\\\s*元\"\n\n# ── ⑤ Implementation parameter override ──\n[ErisPulse.scope.overrides.MyModule.restart]\nmaster = true                 # Only available to framework owner\nhidden = true                 # Hide in help\naliases = [\"rs\"]              # Append alias\nprefix = \"!\"                  # Append trigger prefix\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "2e6f464d847c8bca9678baa97c84557e",
      "translation": "## ① Module Dimension\n\nAnswer: \"In a given context, which modules are available?\" By default, all modules are open; filtering only begins after configuration binding. **No changes are required for modules or adapters.**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Event arrives at a module's handler/command\"] --> B{\"scope.is_allowed<br/>(platform, bot, module, session)\"}\n    B --> C{\"Look up active bindings<br/>Session-level > Bot-level > Platform-level\"}\n    C -->|\"Matched\"| D[\"blocked matched → Reject<br/>modules non-empty → Only allow from whitelist<br/>Both empty → default_allow\"]\n    C -->|\"Not matched\"| E[\"default_allow (default true = allow)\"]\n    D -->|\"Reject\"| Z[\"Silently ignore<br/> (No reply, no claim, only TRACE log visible)\"]\n```\n\n- **Resolution Priority:** Session-level > Bot-level > Platform-level. Higher priority bindings **fully override** lower priority ones.\n- **Silent Semantics:** Commands and handlers of filtered modules are not triggered, replied to, or claimed (to prevent cross-command mis-matches), only visible in TRACE-level logs (`core.scope.denied`).\n- **Framework-level handlers** (`scope_exempt=True` or owner is empty) are unaffected; module names that are empty (framework-level resources) are always allowed."
    },
    {
      "hash": "76ab0a703d7192a9f250a79750e98dfc",
      "translation": "## ② Identity Dimension (Event Admission)\n\nAnswer the question: \"Whose events are accepted or rejected?\" Events that are denied are **completely discarded at the distribution entry**—they do not enter any middleware or processor (including framework-level), and are only visible in TRACE-level logs (`core.scope.identity_denied`).\n\n- **Resolution priority: User > Session > Bot > Adapter**, taking the most specific configured policy; deny takes precedence over allow\n- Each level is bound to a binary policy: `{ allow = true }` or `{ deny = true }`\n- User keys support glob / regular expressions (e.g., `\"spam_*\"` to block a batch of spam users)\n- Typical usage—deny at the upper level and allow for specific users to create \"exceptional passes\":\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.adapters.onebot11]\ndeny = true\n[ErisPulse.scope.identity.users.onebot11]\nallow = [\"u_admin\"]   # Even if the adapter-level policy is to deny, events from u_admin are still allowed\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "9400495605b5d78df72444aa7cf6ba5c",
      "translation": "## ③ Command Dimension (Command ACL)\n\nAnswer the question \"Who can execute a specific command?\" The decision order is as follows: **If deny is hit → deny; if allow whitelist is not empty and not hit → deny; if neither is configured → follow `default_allow`** (`true` gives the default permission chain to developers). Commands that are denied will explicitly reply with \"Insufficient permissions\".\n\n- Command names support glob patterns: `\"roll*\"` covers a group of commands such as `roll` and `roll_dice` with a single rule.\n- Exact keys take precedence over glob keys (`commands.roll` is matched first, and `commands.\"roll*\"` is not checked if the exact match is found).\n- User identifiers use the format `\"platform:user_id\"` (consistent with the framework's owner system).\n- This dimension acts as an additional gate on the user side, and is chained with the command's `master` / `permission` parameters:\n  After ACL passes, the default permission chain declared by developers is still followed."
    },
    {
      "hash": "e33358d1dac070ba3a47efd9e311f43d",
      "translation": "## ④ Processor/Text Dimension\n\nFilter \"what text\" by module: After configuring `pattern` / `regex` for a module, all event handlers of that module only trigger when the text matches (AND with conditions in the code, both must be satisfied). This is suitable for narrowing the trigger scope of a module without modifying its code.\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.handlers.ChatModule]\npattern = \"闲聊*\"     # Event handlers of ChatModule only respond to messages starting with \"闲聊\"\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "6201121b63e281462efa420e280e0e75",
      "translation": "## ⑤ Implement Parameter Overriding\n\nOverride implementation parameters at the **upper level** of module/command registration, without modifying the module code:\n\n```toml\n[ErisPulse.scope.overrides.MyModule.restart]\nmaster = true      # Override to only allow the framework owner\nhidden = true      # Hide from the help list\naliases = [\"rs\"]   #生效别名\n```\n\n> Overriding only modifies **implementation parameters** (master / hidden / aliases / prefix / help / usage, etc.).\n> **Do not disable a command here** — use command-level deny (`scope.commands` or `scope.deny_user()`), to avoid conflicting \"disable\" semantics."
    },
    {
      "hash": "6eddd996afb11ba78dc606313e7dbc66",
      "translation": "## Runtime API\n\n### Module Dimension\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\n# Check permission\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"Chat\")\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"Chat\", \"789012345\")\nsdk.scope.is_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", None)      # Framework-level resource -> True\n\n# Bind / Unbind\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Chat\", \"Tool*\"])\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", blocked=[\"Danger\"])             # Platform-level\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"789012345\", modules=[\"Chat\"])  # Session-level\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Music\"], merge=True)  # Merge\nsdk.scope.bind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", modules=[\"Chat\"], persist=False)  # Runtime only\nsdk.scope.unbind_module(\"onebot11\", \"123456\")\n\n# Query\nsdk.scope.get(\"onebot11\", \"123456\")   # {\"modules\": [\"Chat\"], \"blocked\": []}\n```\n\n### Identity Dimension\n\n```python\n# Check if event is allowed\nsdk.scope.is_identity_allowed(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"group_9\", \"u1\")\n\n# Bind policy (hierarchy determined by parameters: user > session > bot > adapter)\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"u_bad\", deny=True)\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"spam_*\", deny=True)   # glob\nsdk.scope.bind_identity(\"onebot11\", \"123456\", \"group_9\", allow=True)\nsdk.scope.unbind_identity(\"onebot11\", user_id=\"u_bad\")\n\n# Convenient API for user blacklist\nsdk.scope.block_user(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.is_user_blocked(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.get_blocked_users()        # {\"onebot11\": [\"u_bad\"]}\nsdk.scope.unblock_user(\"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\n```\n\n### Command Dimension\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.is_command_allowed(\"roll\", \"onebot11\", \"u1\")\nsdk.scope.allow_user(\"roll*\", \"onebot11\", \"u_vip\")   # Command names support glob\nsdk.scope.deny_user(\"roll*\", \"onebot11\", \"u_bad\")\nsdk.scope.get_acl(\"roll*\")\nsdk.scope.remove_acl(\"roll*\")\n\n# Can also use command system facade (equivalent delegation)\nfrom ErisPulse.Core.Event import command\ncommand.allow_user(\"restart\", \"onebot11\", \"123456\")\n```\n\n### Processor and Override Dimension\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.bind_handler(\"MyModule\", pattern=\"签到*\", regex=r\"\\d+号\")\nsdk.scope.unbind_handler(\"MyModule\")\n\nsdk.scope.override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\", master=True, hidden=True)\nsdk.scope.get_override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\")\nsdk.scope.remove_override(\"MyModule\", \"restart\")\n```\n\n### General\n\n```python\nsdk.scope.list_bindings()   # Full bindings across all five dimensions\nsdk.scope.get_topology()    # Topology of all five dimensions (for Dashboard)\nsdk.scope.get_stats()\n# {\"module_calls\": .., \"module_filtered\": .., \"identity_checks\": .., \"identity_denied\": ..,\n#  \"command_checks\": .., \"command_denied\": .., \"cache_hits\": .., \"cache_misses\": ..}\nsdk.scope.reset_stats()\nsdk.scope.clear()           # Clear all bindings (memory-only)\n```"
    },
    {
      "hash": "a6bc5232fec23d25f8eeabb663b53ece",
      "translation": "## Caching and Hot Updates\n\n- The results of `is_allowed` / `is_identity_allowed` are cached with **LRU caching** (`scope.cache_size` is adjustable),\n  and are automatically invalidated by `bind_*` / `unbind_*` / configuration hot updates (`config.updated` / `config.set`).\n- All dimension configurations take effect **immediately** without requiring a restart.\n- The control plane performs \"per-event\" evaluation without cross-event memory: if the configuration changes, the next event will be evaluated using the new rules."
    },
    {
      "hash": "0476bbc0d35f19b68167fdd153c439e9",
      "translation": "## Frequently Asked Questions and Precautions\n\n### 1. Configuration Hierarchy and Overriding\n\n- Module-level: Session level > Bot level > Platform level, **overall override**. To allow \"Chat on the platform, then add Music at the Bot level\", both must be listed at the Bot level.\n- Identity-level: User > Session > Bot > Adapter, the **most specific** configured policy is used (exceptions can be allowed).\n- Command-level: Exact command names take precedence over glob keys.\n\n### 2. Use the Control Plane Instead of Modifying Module Code\n\nModules declare \"developer defaults\" (`master=True`, `permission=...`, `pattern=...`); the control plane declares \"user final decisions\". When conflicts occur, the **more restrictive control plane** takes precedence (e.g., if the developer does not set master, the user can override `master = true` to tighten restrictions; the user cannot loosen the developer's explicit restrictions through overrides—disable/allow control goes through the command deny / identity allow).\n\n### 3. Module/Command Not Responding\n\nFirst suspect the control plane rather than the module itself:\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\nprint(sdk.scope.is_allowed(event.get_platform(), bot_id, \"MyModule\", session_id))\nprint(sdk.scope.is_identity_allowed(event.get_platform(), bot_id, session_id, user_id))\nprint(sdk.scope.get_stats())   # module_filtered / identity_denied > 0 indicates silent filtering\n```\n\nFiltering is **silent** (no reply at the module and identity levels to avoid exposing rules), but statistics are accumulated; command-level ACL rejection will explicitly reply with \"insufficient permissions\".\n\n### 4. Session Identifiers Are Isolated Across Platforms\n\nThe combination of `(platform, session_id)` is the unique identifier. `scope.sessions.onebot11.\"789\"` only applies to onebot11 and does not affect the session with the same `789` on telegram. The same applies to user keys at the identity level."
    },
    {
      "hash": "78e2f3a4867d57014620cc9163a93f17",
      "translation": "## Topology Tree API\n\n`ModuleManager.get_topology()` and `AdapterManager.get_topology()` provide data on module/adapter ownership relationships. `sdk.get_topology()` aggregates (including control plane `scope` with five dimensions) in one call:\n\n```python\nfrom ErisPulse import sdk\n\ntopology = sdk.get_topology()\n# {\n#   \"modules\": {                                   # Module → Owned resources\n#     \"Chat\": {\n#       \"loaded\": True, \"enabled\": True,\n#       \"commands\": [\"chat\", \"translate\"],\n#       \"handlers\": {\"message\": 2, \"notice\": 1},\n#       \"routes\": {\"http\": [\"/Chat/api\"], \"ws\": [], \"sse\": []},\n#       \"lifecycle_hooks\": 3,\n#     }\n#   },\n#   \"adapters\": {                                  # Adapter → Bot → Scope\n#     \"onebot11\": {\n#       \"status\": \"started\", \"enabled\": True,\n#       \"bots\": {\"123456\": {\"status\": \"online\", \"scope\": {...}}},\n#       \"scope\": {\"modules\": [...], \"blocked\": [...]},\n#     }\n#   },\n#   \"scope\": {                                     # Unified control plane (five dimensions)\n#     \"platforms\": {...}, \"bots\": {...}, \"sessions\": {...},\n#     \"identity\": {\"adapters\": {...}, \"bots\": {...}, \"sessions\": {...}, \"users\": {...}},\n#     \"commands\": {...}, \"handlers\": {...}, \"overrides\": {...},\n#   },\n# }\n```\n\n- The module topology aggregates commands, event handlers, HTTP/WS/SSE routes, and lifecycle hooks registered by the module, making it easier to visualize the module resource tree.\n- The adapter topology aggregates the status of each adapter, the status of its subordinate Bots, and platform-level/Bot-level scope bindings."
    }
  ]
}